+# The Daily Grind origin migration
+
+The source is a static site. No server is required to build it. Migration is
+disabled in the unconfigured source so it never sends preferences to an example
+or inferred destination.
+
+## Configure and build
+
+Supply the actual origins, both HTTPS and sharing a registrable domain. The
+builder uses a Public Suffix List, including private domains, so unrelated
+tenants such as `one.github.io` and `two.github.io` are rejected.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+OLD_ORIGIN=https://old.example.com \
+NEW_ORIGIN=https://app.example.com \
+OLD_APP_ID=/ \
+MIGRATION_STORAGE_KEYS='[]' \
+pnpm build
+```
+
+The example URLs are placeholders. `OLD_APP_ID` must match the **existing
+installed app's resolved manifest ID**, including any query string. If the old
+manifest omitted `id`, use its resolved `start_url`. Do not assign a new ID to
+an existing installation. This project has no prior manifest or saved settings;
+the defaults assume a root installation and no configuration keys. Confirm
+these against the deployed app before rollout.
+
+`MIGRATION_STORAGE_KEYS` is an explicit JSON list of localStorage keys holding
+user configuration, for example `["daily-grind:preferences"]`. Include the
+actual keys from the deployed app. Do not include authentication tokens or
+session data. Both builds use exactly the same allowlist. Missing source values
+are skipped and existing destination values take priority. A failed write rolls
+back newly copied values; source settings are never deleted. No storage keys are
+enumerated or exported implicitly.
+
+## Deploy both sides
+
+1. Deploy **dist/new/** at the destination origin's root. It includes a stable
+   manifest `id` and `migrate_from` pointing to the old installed identity.
+2. Deploy **dist/old/** at the old origin's root. It includes `migrate_to` and
+   `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` authorizing the destination ID.
+   Keep the association response available without authentication or redirects
+   and serve it as `application/json`. Serve manifests as
+   `application/manifest+json`. Include the hidden `.well-known` directory.
+3. Keep the old origin, its manifest, and `/transfer.html` accessible while users
+   migrate. Do not blanket-redirect the old origin: the settings popup must run
+   there to read the original origin's storage. Avoid stale long-lived caching
+   of manifests, the association response, and `migration-config.js`.
+4. Preserve the popup's opener relationship. A restrictive
+   `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin` header or an opener-removing hosting
+   policy breaks the handoff. The pages need to communicate across these two
+   exact origins. The flow uses an explicit user click on each site, verifies
+   the sender origin and window, and correlates messages to a random request ID.
+
+On the destination, “Bring my settings” opens the old origin. The user approves
+the copy there. Keep this available even after an installation update: origin
+migration itself does not copy localStorage.
+
+Supporting browsers offer the native app update, preserving installation
+integration such as OS shortcuts. No JavaScript API can force that update or
+guarantee shortcut preservation in unsupported browsers. The old-site banner
+provides the fallback: visit the destination, copy settings, then manually
+install the destination using the browser menu if no native update is offered.
+The code does not attempt unreliable feature detection for manifest members.
+
+Cookies, IndexedDB, service-worker caches, and browser permissions are not
+transferred by this implementation. This source app uses none of them. If the
+deployed app stores configuration there, add a specific serialization adapter
+or server-side account synchronization before migrating it. Permissions must
+be requested again as needed. OS integration is managed by the browser; this
+repository alone cannot migrate an installed app without both deployments.
+
+The [`migrate_to` object and destination-ID association key](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration)
+follow Chrome's published migration documentation. Browser support is limited;
+do not promise native migration to every user.
+
+## Static validation
+
+Run `node --check migration.js`, `node --check scripts/build.mjs`, and the
+configured build. Inspect both generated manifests, the association file, and
+the two origin allowlists before deployment. These checks do not start a server
+or import a server entrypoint.
